Ontology)","study_design":"MeSH Publication Types","strength":"Oxford CEBM 2009 levels","structure":"SEPIO assertion / evidence line / evidence item"},"search_url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=trans-Pinocarveol%5BAll%20Fields%5D"},"summary":null,"evidence":"Measured in 2 of 6 MONDAYS hemp SKUs at 0.05–0.09% of total volatiles (0.01–0.018mg per chew).","trials":{"total":0,"matched_loosely":0,"studies":[],"source":"ClinicalTrials.gov","search_url":"https://clinicaltrials.gov/search?intr=trans-Pinocarveol"},"is_terpenoid":false,"consumer_summary":{"model":"gemini-3.1-pro-preview","generated":"2026-09-11","what_it_is":"trans-Pinocarveol is a fragrant, volatile molecule known as a monoterpene alcohol, responsible for some of the fresh, woody, and herbal aromas found in nature. People most commonly encounter it when smelling the crisp scent of pine needles, the cooling vapor of eucalyptus, or when brewing certain traditional herbal teas.","found_in":"It is naturally produced by a wide variety of aromatic plants, including sweet wormwood (Artemisia annua), Korean pine, eucalyptus trees, and even the revered Reishi mushroom (Ganoderma lucidum).","why_researchers_care":"Researchers are drawn to trans-pinocarveol because it frequently appears as a key component in essential oils traditionally used to fight infections and soothe inflammation. Scientists are particularly interested in its potential as a natural antimicrobial and antioxidant agent. The strongest evidence so far comes from human observational studies (Oxford level 2b) examining complex plant extracts where trans-pinocarveol plays a starring role, prompting further investigation into how this specific molecule behaves on its own.","structure_function":[{"claim_id":"antimicrobial","statement":"Early observational work points toward a role in supporting the body's natural defenses against microbes.","evidence_level":"human_observational","basis":"Based on 9 papers, including human observational studies and lab research.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0000180","label":"clinical study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"2b","label":"individual cohort or observational study"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"human_observational","eco":"ECO:0000180","papers":4},{"design":"in_vitro","eco":"ECO:0000181","papers":2}],"paper_count":9},{"claim_id":"antioxidant","statement":"May support cellular health by acting as a natural antioxidant.","evidence_level":"human_observational","basis":"Based on 4 papers, including human observational and animal research.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0000180","label":"clinical study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"2b","label":"individual cohort or observational study"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"human_observational","eco":"ECO:0000180","papers":3},{"design":"animal","eco":"ECO:0007120","papers":1}],"paper_count":4},{"claim_id":"anti-inflammatory","statement":"Early animal work points toward a potential ability to help modulate inflammatory responses.","evidence_level":"animal","basis":"Based on 2 papers, including animal research.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0007120","label":"animal model system study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"5","label":"bench or animal research"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"animal","eco":"ECO:0007120","papers":1}],"paper_count":2},{"claim_id":"cancer","statement":"Is being studied for its potential role in cellular health and abnormal cell growth.","evidence_level":"human_observational","basis":"Based on 2 human observational papers.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0000180","label":"clinical study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"2b","label":"individual cohort or observational study"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"human_observational","eco":"ECO:0000180","papers":2}],"paper_count":2},{"claim_id":"insect","statement":"May act as a natural deterrent against insects.","evidence_level":"human_observational","basis":"Based on 2 papers, including human observational and animal data.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0000180","label":"clinical study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"2b","label":"individual cohort or observational study"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"human_observational","eco":"ECO:0000180","papers":1},{"design":"animal","eco":"ECO:0007120","papers":1}],"paper_count":2}],"hypothesized_benefits":[{"claim_id":"antimicrobial","area":"Infection Control","hypothesis":"Researchers are exploring whether trans-pinocarveol can effectively neutralize harmful bacteria and fungi, which could make it a valuable natural preservative or topical agent if it bears out.","evidence_level":"human_observational","paper_count":9,"study_to_test_it":"A randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled human trial testing a standardized trans-pinocarveol topical cream against a placebo for mild bacterial skin colonization over two weeks.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0000180","label":"clinical study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"2b","label":"individual cohort or observational study"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"human_observational","eco":"ECO:0000180","papers":4},{"design":"in_vitro","eco":"ECO:0000181","papers":2}]},{"claim_id":"anti-inflammatory","area":"Inflammation Modulation","hypothesis":"Early animal work points toward the possibility that this molecule can suppress inflammatory markers like elastase, which would be highly relevant for soothing skin or respiratory irritation.","evidence_level":"animal","paper_count":2,"study_to_test_it":"A Phase I/II human clinical trial measuring inflammatory biomarkers in the blood and sputum of healthy volunteers exposed to inhaled trans-pinocarveol vapor over 14 days.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0007120","label":"animal model system study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"5","label":"bench or animal research"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"animal","eco":"ECO:0007120","papers":1}]},{"claim_id":"antioxidant","area":"Cellular Protection","hypothesis":"Scientists are investigating if trans-pinocarveol can protect cells from oxidative stress, potentially supporting long-term tissue health.","evidence_level":"human_observational","paper_count":4,"study_to_test_it":"A ra